# Kestrel Yard — an assessment, in the built-up end of a works town.
#
# The second mission, on the second map, and a reconnaissance again on purpose: the format is
# argued from two shapes of ground carrying one shape of mission, so what differs between the two
# files is the ground and not the objective. The briefing is the mission book's own example for a
# reconnaissance (docs/setting/missions.md section 4), fitted to what got drawn.

mission Kestrel Yard: an assessment
map kestrel

# ---- the briefing, in the mission book's six parts ------------------------------------------

brief instrument  An assessment of the Cadre holding at Kestrel Yard, ahead of the arbitration.

brief presence    We believe plate is coming through the yard in quantity. We do not know how much.
brief presence    The gate on the main street has a man on it all night, and the office across the
brief presence    east lane has somebody at the upstairs window who can see over the wall into the
brief presence    yard. One of them sleeps in the shed with whatever is in it, or the last report
brief presence    thinks so. There is a set on the site and nobody has seen who carries it.

brief task        Get eyes on the loading floor at the east end of the shed. Properly on it —
brief task        traced, not glimpsed through the door from the street.

brief restraint   There is a night shift in the terrace across the street. They are not ours and
brief restraint   they are not theirs and you will leave them entirely alone.

brief way-off     Off over the footbridge across the cut, north-west of the yard, before the shift
brief way-off     changes.

brief stop        If they know you were there, they will move it, and the assessment is worth
brief stop        nothing before you are home. Being shot at is survivable. Being identified is the
brief stop        failure.

# ---- named ground ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Four places, and two of them are what the waystation could not test. The loading floor is a
# building interior rather than a patch of open ground, and it is inside another place, the shed.
# The footbridge is upstairs: the exit is three metres up, over water, and the only way onto it is
# the steps from the towpath.

place shed          block -7,13 to 4,0
place loading-floor block -1,5 to 4,5
place yard          block -8,-4 to 5,-1
place footbridge    line -10,13 to -12,14 layer 1

# ---- who is standing where ------------------------------------------------------------------

# Ours, in the east lane at the end of the main street, round the corner from the gate behind the
# weighbridge hut. Out of docs/setting/roster.md section 4, and none of them the three who went
# to the waystation: Tolliver is the scout who goes and looks at things that might look back,
# Idris the silent carbine, and Naismith the set, because section 7 says one is compulsory.
deploy Tolliver player 13,-12 facing nw role scout     kit beamer
deploy Idris    player 12,-12 facing nw                kit beamer
deploy Naismith player 13,-13 facing nw role signaller kit beamer

# Theirs, out of section 5 — four of the Cadre's twelve, none of the four the waystation fields,
# which fits a detail that rotates through the sites on its stretch of road. Duvall is the answer
# to a door and has the gate. Sivet, who is the one who will notice if anybody does, has the
# office window, which is a deployment that has to name a storey. Ganz has the second set and is
# kept out of the obvious place, which here is downstairs in the office. Rask holds the detail and
# sleeps beside what the detail is guarding.
deploy Duvall hostile 0,-4   facing s  role trooper   kit heavy
deploy Sivet  hostile 7,-3@1 facing sw role scout     kit rifleman
deploy Ganz   hostile 9,-3   facing sw role signaller kit sidearm
deploy Rask   hostile 2,4    facing s                 kit rifleman

# The night shift in the terrace is not deployed. Side.Neutral exists and nothing makes a neutral
# behave like a person (docs/setting.md section 5), so a worker here would be a figure standing
# in a house with the door shut, which is what the terrace already is.

# ---- what the squad is told -----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The gate and the window are stated flat. The shed is "or the last report thinks so". The set is
# on the site and nobody has placed it, so Ganz is told about not at all — a post the reports
# missed is the one thing a 'told' line says by being absent.
told player searching  Duvall Sivet
told player suspicious Rask

# ---- what we came to do ---------------------------------------------------------------------

# Being shot at is survivable and being identified is the failure, so a departing soldier may be
# held as high as Searching and no higher: Alerted is the rung at which a contact is passed on,
# and a contact passed on is an identification. The waystation's briefing is stricter — Suspicious
# — because its stop is "properly registers you", and the difference is the briefing's to say.
objective reconnaissance player at loading-floor exit footbridge unnoticed searching

# Before the shift changes. Twenty rounds against the waystation's thirty, on a map a little over
# half as far across; the approach here is one decision long, not two. The alarm half is left
# unwritten for the same reason as the waystation's — it is the playtest's to set.
rounds 20
